# SLVROV Web UI Design Guide

This guide turns the collected UI advice into project-specific rules for the
MVP website demos. Use it when creating static demos, production frontend
structure, and later mode pages.

## 1. Product Intent

- Design for operators who need to drive an ROV safely, configure it without
  developer knowledge, and diagnose issues quickly.
- Build the actual tool UI first. Do not create a marketing landing page.
- Keep the MVP focused on three modes: Pilot, Setup, and Developer.
- Treat camera visibility, safety state, and clear operator feedback as higher
  priority than decoration.
- Use progressive disclosure: show the controls needed for the current task,
  then reveal advanced details only when the user asks for them.

## 2. Global Interaction Rules

- Every interactive element must have a clear signifier: button affordance,
  hover state, pressed/active state, disabled state, and loading/error feedback.
- Use icons with labels where an action could be ambiguous. Use icon-only
  buttons only for familiar actions and include tooltips.
- Prefer Lucide icons for interface actions when available.
- Avoid decorative emojis.
- Use optimistic UI only for low-risk changes. Safety actions, test starts,
  motor actions, and destructive operations must wait for explicit confirmation
  and show authoritative state after the backend responds.
- Keyboard shortcuts are useful for Developer Mode and repeated Setup tasks,
  but do not rely on them for safety-critical actions.
- Do not use drag-and-drop for MVP camera layouts or setup workflows; fixed
  presets are easier to test and safer to explain.

## 3. Layout And Navigation

- Use predictable web-app conventions: top-level mode navigation, clear page
  titles, left-to-right and top-to-bottom scanning, and stable controls.
- Pilot Mode should minimize chrome and maximize camera area.
- Setup Mode should feel procedural: grouped steps, tabs, or a guided flow.
- Developer Mode can be denser, but it must remain organized and scannable.
- Sidebars are allowed only when they reduce cognitive load. Avoid a sidebar
  that simply repeats the top navigation.
- Use a consistent 4-point spacing grid. Components may adapt responsively, but
  spacing should feel intentional.
- Define stable dimensions for camera tiles, status bars, toolbar buttons,
  tabs, cards, and test-result rows so state changes do not shift the layout.
- Do not nest UI cards inside other cards. Use cards for repeated items,
  modals, and framed tools only.

## 4. Visual Hierarchy

- Important safety and mode-state information must be visible without reading
  paragraphs.
- Use size, position, contrast, and color to guide attention. Do not use color
  alone to communicate state.
- Keep the most important action or status near the top of each mode view.
- Use concise text. Avoid visible instructional copy that explains obvious UI
  mechanics; the interface should communicate through structure and states.
- Use empty states for missing cameras, disconnected services, no test results,
  and unavailable developer data.

## 5. Color And Theme

- Dark theme is the default.
- Use neutral dark backgrounds with slightly lighter surfaces for depth; avoid
  pure black except where it improves camera contrast.
- Use semantic colors consistently:
  - Red: emergency stop, critical alerts, destructive actions.
  - Yellow/amber: warnings and caution states.
  - Green: healthy/success states.
  - Blue/cyan: neutral active selection, links, or live/connected status.
- Keep accent color usage sparse. Status, active tabs, and critical actions
  should carry most of the color.
- Do not create a one-note palette dominated by a single hue family.
- Check contrast for all text, icons, controls, and camera overlays.
- Never place text over camera imagery without a readable backing treatment
  such as a solid/translucent surface or gradient.

## 6. Typography

- Use one high-quality sans-serif font stack.
- Keep letter spacing at `0`; do not use negative tracking.
- Use a limited type scale. Dashboard/tool surfaces should use compact,
  readable headings rather than hero-scale text.
- Use line heights that support scanning: tighter for headings, comfortable for
  body text and table rows.
- Ensure long labels, device names, and status strings wrap or truncate cleanly
  without overlapping controls.

## 7. Components And Feedback

- Buttons need clear hover, pressed, disabled, loading, success, and error
  states.
- Segmented controls fit mode/layout choices.
- Tabs fit Setup sections and Developer subviews.
- Toggles fit enabled/disabled camera or config settings.
- Sliders/steppers/number inputs fit tunable numeric values.
- Tables/lists fit Developer diagnostics and setup summaries; include search,
  filter, or sort only when the data volume justifies it.
- Toasts can confirm low-risk saves. Critical safety alerts should use
  persistent banners or panels, not transient toasts.
- Modals are appropriate for confirmations and complex focused tasks.
- Popovers are appropriate for simple display settings or secondary actions.

## 8. Mode-Specific Guidance

### Pilot Mode

- Camera-first layout. Keep controls sparse and out of the camera path.
- Emergency stop and critical alerts must remain visible in every camera layout.
- Provide selected single-camera and fixed multi-camera presets.
- Use clear unavailable/offline camera placeholders.
- Avoid telemetry clutter unless a later task explicitly approves it.

### Setup Mode

- Organize camera setup, control mapping, thruster configuration, and preflight
  into a guided flow or clear tabs.
- Show progress and saved/unsaved state.
- Make dangerous motor-test actions explicit, confirmed, low-power, timed, and
  cancelable.
- Favor forms with grouped fields, validation messages, and clear save/revert
  behavior.

### Developer Mode

- Dense is acceptable; chaotic is not.
- Use compact stat cards, lists, and tables with clear healthy/warning/error
  states.
- Allow developers to choose visible stats where useful.
- Keep dangerous actions allowlisted and confirmation-gated.
- Do not expose arbitrary ROS2 publish/service-call tools in MVP demos unless
  the task explicitly asks for that risk.

## 9. Demo Requirements

- Every demo must be clearly labeled as non-production.
- Provide enough fake data to judge layout, density, empty states, warnings,
  and critical alerts.
- Include at least one offline/error state in each mode demo.
- Check 4:3 and 16:10-ish large-display viewports.
- Verify text, controls, camera tiles, alert banners, and emergency stop
  controls do not overlap.
- Include short tradeoff notes for each demo option so the owner can choose
  based on usability, not just taste.
- Keep unselected demos disposable or clearly archived after the owner chooses
  a direction.
